## Audit conclusion

Phase 1.5 is **complete-frozen**. A live re-run of `npm run phase15:audit` against current `main` (commit `7c5eff1736612d1e33dc4082c959e6bf61ce9f1d`, observed `2026-08-11T05:30:35Z`) returned:

```json
{
  "phase": "1.5",
  "status": "complete-frozen",
  "localReady": true,
  "publicationReady": true,
  "externalReady": true,
  "failedLocalGates": [],
  "failedPublicationGates": [],
  "failedExternalGates": [],
  "closureSentence": "Phase 1.5 closed. Beacon may proceed to the next business-domain/UI phase."
}
```

This is the literal command output, not a paraphrase; per Section 9/29A of the Phase 1.6 master spec, this observation is timestamped evidence, not a durable fact — re-run the command before relying on it in a later session. All local, publication, and external gates are true and `failedLocalGates`/`failedPublicationGates`/`failedExternalGates` are all empty.

`npm run phase15:audit:local` reports only `localReady`. `npm run ci:prepublish` produces `publicationReady` for the exact candidate. `npm run phase15:audit` intentionally exits nonzero until `localReady`, `publicationReady`, and `externalReady` are all true; it now exits zero and emits the closure sentence above.

`localReady` never authorizes publication. `publicationReady` never claims GitHub/provider/deployment evidence. Per ADR-0018, evidence is bound to the candidate's git tree content hash, not its commit SHA — a real GitHub merge always produces a new commit SHA, so commit-SHA equality could never survive a real merge; a changed tree still invalidates evidence, but a merge-generated SHA change alone does not. The `.github/workflows/pr-prepublish.yml` workflow now runs `ci:prepublish` on the pull request itself as a real pre-merge CI job (job id `prepublish`); adding that check context to the branch-protection ruleset and enabling "require branches up to date before merging" remains explicit follow-up work, not yet done.

